(H/'classification.md').write_text('''# Classification at basis 2275

The exact source wrapper contains 12 practice questions: 5 easy, 5 moderate, no hard, and 2 unrated. Only one easy and one moderate question carry Math Academy IDs. Eight rated records are captured non-MA inventory, kept separately from newly authored variants. The canonical example is evidence of method and is excluded from practice counts.

Easy source q-71829 asks to identify the points satisfying y=4x+2 from A(-1,-2), B(0,1), C(1,6). One radio field `selection` has AC,A,B,C,BC with correct AC. Its solution reads the coordinates, states the substitution criterion, tests each point with three aligned equation rows and a truth mark, gives a per-point conclusion, then the final matching subset. The four captured non-MA easy records have the identical backbone and whole bank with different integer coefficients/coordinates; classification.json retains each exact prompt, solution, field and every choice.

Moderate source q-102142 supplies y=-5x+6 and A(0,5), B(1,2), C(2,-4). Its `selection` radio bank is AC,C,AB,B,A with correct C. The coordinate-list punctuation, A's emphasized *not*, question wording and substitution prose differ from easy. It is a separate source template. Four non-MA moderate records preserve that same structure.

Unrated q-22405 and q-44504 have distinct prompts, three field-owned checkbox true/false banks, and short coordinate/only-B worked solutions. Their original omission of difficulty is preserved. They are not assigned a guessed level and are not normal generated difficulty templates. All six Boolean banks replay in full.

Every prompt contains a central point-only Cartesian plot; plot rating is heavy. Source assets are captured locally and were opened as a contact sheet and individually where needed. No line should be drawn: membership is determined by coordinate reading and substitution. No source mathematical error was identified.
''')
(H/'template-extraction.md').write_text('''# Template extraction and slots

At original parameters, all 12 records replay their exact original prompt, worked solution, answer fields, complete banks, keys and IDs. Source-cases.json contains only these original wrappers. The formatter normalizes enum/reference representation without inventing omitted attributes. The 62 observed choice values, including 16 complete field banks, are mapped in choice-ledger.json. Full raw evidence remains source.json.

The E/M backbone is: read three distinct labeled integer grid points; supply y=mx+b; test A, B and C by substituting both coordinates; compare the resulting equality; state each membership; select the exact subset in one five-choice radio field. Fixed method: substitution into the supplied line; three points; three equation rows per point; shared explanatory sentences; final subset. Variable numeric slots: easy m in {1,2,3,4}, moderate m in {-2,-3,-4,-5}, b in -4..6, three distinct x coordinates in -4..4, nonmember integer vertical deviations of magnitude 1/2, all displayed y coordinates in -10..10. Explicit source replay retains all source values. Independent numeric slots do not depend on a hardcoded target label.

Variable role slots: the easy source has two member roles and one nonmember role; moderate has one member and two nonmember roles. Choose one complete source-bank role permutation before sampling numbers. Map every source option as a complete label-subset object through that same bijection. The three member-subset possibilities AB/AC/BC (easy) or A/B/C (moderate) are eligible and have the same arithmetic method/workload. Labels stay in left-to-right x order in normal plots; changing the member role changes actual y coordinates rather than cosmetically relabeling an unchanged question. Each reference in summary, substitution operand, result, truth marker, per-point conclusion, bank/key and final subset derives from those roles. Retaining original AC/C merely because examples repeat it would be unjustified.

Source whole-bank profile E is {AC,A,B,C,BC}; M is {AC,C,AB,B,A}. Selecting a profile plus bijection selects all five options before coefficients. An option recipe is a selected subset of original roles, not a guessed numeric error. Each profile is reproduced completely, and the full subset objects must be distinct with exactly one mathematically correct option. The validator exercises all six permutations independently. A template ID chooses source wording, never a pooled distractor list. The eight captured non-MA rows remain independent source inventory and have their own explicit original replay cases.

Authored missing-hard extension: use a nonintegral rational slope with reduced denominator 2 or 3 and numerator from -5..5 excluding zero, integer intercept -4..6 and integer grid coordinates. Keep the moderate source's same three substitution/equality stages and complete radio subset-bank roles. Integer x values for the member point are constrained so its y coordinate is integral; nonmembers remain integral grid points. Fraction arithmetic is the sole added workload. This is a disclosed conservative same-method extension, not observed hard fidelity and not a claim of calibrated Math Academy difficulty. No systems, finding slopes, fitting a line, solving for unknown coordinates, graph construction or new reasoning branch is introduced.

Canonical dedup uses the actual linear equation and unordered supplied coordinate set plus point-membership task, ignoring labels, bank profile, template, IDs, seed and plot path. Normal sampling accepts all valid tasks in that declared domain; no filtering against prior source math is built into the sampler. Batch accepts an explicit optional set of already-seen task hashes. Content UUIDs additionally include the complete bank to prevent different bank objects sharing an identity. Original records retain original IDs. Strict built-in int seed/count guards reject booleans, strings, fractional inputs and invalid or excessive counts; bounded resampling raises on exhaustion. 10000 is a conservative per-level request cap, not an exact capacity theorem.
''')
(H/'lesson-scope.md').write_text('''# Lesson route and scope

Frozen topic.json and lesson.json are read at basis 2275. The Graphing Linear Equations activity starts at step 17592186055668 (Introduction). The next chain visits table plotting, a tutorial about two points determining a line, sketching a line using two points, and identifying a line from a graph; its last step is this point-membership KP (17592186055637). The topic follows Two-Variable Linear Equations and Their Solutions; subsequent topics include systems, reflections and horizontal/vertical lines. The canonical point-membership example substitutes A(0,5), B(2,1), C(3,0) into y=5-2x and tests each equality.

The assessed operation here is coordinate reading plus direct substitution into an already supplied equation. Neighboring plotting/sketching lessons do not authorize moving those tasks into these questions. The authored H variants keep point selection and substitution intact while adding only fraction arithmetic; H is a qualified authored difficulty extension because the captured practice bank has no hard examples. Source unrated checkbox questions remain unrated originals.
''')
(H/'plot-extraction.md').write_text('''# Plot evidence and renderer

reference/assets.json maps all 13 original images (12 practice plus canonical) to their exact source references, local copies and question IDs. Original MA images use gray square unit grids, black axes/positive arrowheads and italic A/B/C labels beside red points; non-MA images use equivalent point-only grids with ordinary labels and integer ticks. Original source image dimensions and hashes are recorded separately. No prompt contains a drawn target line; no answer or worked-solution plot is required. The plot is central to every task, rating heavy.

Read coordinates: canonical A(0,5),B(2,1),C(3,0); q-22405 A(2,7),B(4,5),C(7,2); q-44504 A(-6,5),B(1,5),C(6,5); q-71829 A(-1,-2),B(0,1),C(1,6); q-102142 A(0,5),B(1,2),C(2,-4). The eight non-MA coordinate sets are explicitly retained in classification.json and were checked against source-contact.png. All source points and keys agree.

Normal variants use Matplotlib on a 5-inch square canvas at 120 dpi, equal axis units, gray integer grid, zero-crossing black axes with positive arrowheads, red markers at zorder 5 (including on axes), and readable A/B/C labels with white backing. Points, labels, bounds and grid derive from the exact same parameter instance; no independent plot sampling. x coordinates are ordered in normal sampling. Bounds include every point and the origin with margins. Integer ticks, upper axis-label placement and plot proportions are visual modernizations of the sources with the same instructional purpose. Exact original replays reuse exact original assets.

Every new plot has a reproducible parameter/render-revision hash filename and a rendering sidecar recording bounds, marker coordinates, label pixel boxes and renderer versions. Label boxes must fit the canvas. Independent PNG readback infers the axes and gray-grid unit spacing from pixels and reads all three red-marker centers, comparing them to the coordinates read from actual worked prose; it does not trust the sidecar. A stale-plot negative control keeps the final key/solution correct and must fail that pixel check. Source/normal screenshots are checked for readable labels, clipping, equal scales and no drawn answer line.
''')
(H/'README.md').write_text('''# Point-line draft review bundle

Run `.venv/bin/python -B generate/scratch/17592186054676-draft.py --difficulty all --count 1 --seed 2275 --format json` for a list of three E/M/H records; `--format markdown` displays prompt, full bank and solution. `generate(level, seed, template, parameters, bank_profile)` permits an exact source replay using its original parameters and `LEVEL-source-bank:ABC`; `replay_raw(source_id)` retains all original source records including difficulty omissions and checkbox fields. Normal E/M/H use radio fields. Hard is an explicitly authored rational-slope extension with source-shaped substitution stages.

`validate.py` independently reads actual printed equations, coordinate summaries, substitution operands, evaluated equalities, truth marks, every per-point/final conclusion and complete mathematical subset banks; it reads normal plot coordinates directly from PNG pixels. It exercises all role profiles, holds numeric line/x slots fixed while changing member positions, and runs ten corruption probes with otherwise-correct final keys where possible. Native grading runs the frozen actual check-engine binary on every original and reviewed choice. Unsupported literal-label submissions are retained as explicit unresolved contract probes; algebraic free-entry equivalence is inapplicable to these selection-only fields.

`browser_qa.py` renders all source and review records, including all choices and all solution stages, through a frozen copy of the actual player's Markdown, DOMPurify and MathJax pipeline. Full rendered math trees, layout checks and representative full-record screenshots are retained. It needs a local browser/localhost socket. No external page is accessed.

source.json is the exact basis-2275 wrapper. source-cases.json contains original-ID wrappers only; independent-inventory.json contains only the eight captured non-MA records with lineage and string relationship descriptions. Authored variants live in review-cases.json. classification.json preserves all source components; extraction/scope documents explain slots, profiles, source differences and authored H limits.

Registered numeric.py and its entire 143-file tree are immutable. Their initial full-path byte hashes were captured before generator imports in reference/registered-baseline.json; validation checks both the complete path set and every byte hash. No numeric module is imported. The draft imports only its frozen formatter, source wrapper and installed dependencies. The actual engine binary/source and UI pipeline are frozen privately; no shared engine, registry, queue, database or source changes occur. freeze.py records the whole owned tree and generator, excluding its own hash manifest and summary only.
''')
